## Claim
Text: "UK Employees unable to work in tier 3 areas will get a combination of Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit, which will mean they get 80% of their wages."
Claim author: Boris Johnson
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did the UK government announce on or before October 21, 2020, a policy that employees in Tier 3 areas who are unable to work would receive a combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit?
Answer: Yes, the UK government announced this policy on October 21, 2020, when Prime Minister Boris Johnson stated at Prime Minister's Questions that "Combine the Universal Credit with the Job Support Scheme that we've just announced and workers will be getting 80% of their existing salary." However, according to the BBC's fact-check, the claim is accurate only for some low-income workers, as not all employees qualify for Universal Credit or receive enough to reach the 80% figure.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### What percentage of an employee's usual wages was the Job Support Scheme designed to cover for employees unable to work in Tier 3 areas as of October 21, 2020?
Answer: The Job Support Scheme was designed to cover 67% of an employee's usual wages for those unable to work in Tier 3 areas, up to a maximum of £2,100 per month.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### Did the combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit guarantee that an employee unable to work in a Tier 3 area would receive exactly 80% of their wages, or was 80% a maximum or average figure?
Answer: Based on the BBC article, the 80% figure was not a guaranteed exact amount for all employees; rather, it was a maximum or best-case scenario for some workers, particularly those on very low incomes who qualified for full Universal Credit. The article states that "some workers will get less than 80% under the new scheme" and provides examples, such as a single person earning £11,000 a year who would end up with 73% of their usual income. Therefore, the claim that the combination guarantees exactly 80% is inaccurate.

