### How was Universal Credit calculated and integrated with the Job Support Scheme to achieve the stated 80% wage replacement for employees in Tier 3 areas?
Answer: According to the BBC's fact-check, the Job Support Scheme provides 67% of normal salary up to £2,100 a month, and Universal Credit (UC) is added on top for eligible workers. The integration achieves 80% wage replacement only for those on low incomes who qualify for the full UC entitlement (i.e., with no reductions for savings). The Department for Work and Pensions stated that "those on low incomes getting the full entitlement [of Universal Credit] will receive at least 80% of their normal income." However, eligibility for UC depends on factors such as income level, savings (with reductions starting at £6,000 and no eligibility above £16,000), and whether the worker has children. Therefore, the 80% figure is not universal; many workers, especially those with higher incomes or savings, will receive less.

