## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of key insights
The fact-check confirms that on October 21, 2020, Boris Johnson stated that combining the Job Support Scheme (JSS) and Universal Credit (UC) would give workers in Tier 3 areas 80% of their wages. However, the evidence shows this is accurate only for a subset of low-income workers who qualify for full UC. The JSS itself covers 67% of wages (capped at £2,100/month), and UC adds to that, but eligibility for UC depends on income, savings, and other factors. Many workers—such as those with savings over £6,000, higher earners, or those without children—receive less than 80%. Additionally, the JSS expansion applied only to businesses legally required to close, not all Tier 3 areas. The claim thus presents a best-case scenario as a universal guarantee, omitting crucial limitations.

### Step 2: No further investigation needed
The evidence is clear and sufficient to reach a decision; no additional information is required.

### Step 3: Conclusion
The claim is technically true for some low-income workers who meet all eligibility criteria, but it misleads by implying that all employees unable to work in Tier 3 areas would receive 80% of their wages. The omitted context—such as eligibility restrictions, caps, and the fact that many workers get less—creates a significantly different impression. Therefore, the appropriate decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The fact-check shows that Boris Johnson's statement that combining the Job Support Scheme (JSS) and Universal Credit (UC) would give employees in Tier 3 areas 80% of their wages is accurate only for some low-income workers who qualify for full UC, not for all employees [BBC News](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147). The JSS covers 67% of wages up to £2,100/month, but UC eligibility depends on income, savings, and other factors, meaning many workers—such as those with savings over £6,000 or higher earners—receive less than 80% [BBC News](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147). Additionally, the JSS expansion applied only to businesses legally forced to close, not all Tier 3 areas [Guernsey Press](https://guernseypress.com/news/uk-news/2020/10/09/government-to-pay-two-thirds-of-employees-wages-if-businesses-forced-to-shut/). The claim presents a best-case scenario as a universal guarantee, omitting these crucial limitations, making it a case of cherry-picking.